  "rights-duties-japanese-subjects":japanLawArticle({
    title:"Rights and Duties of Japanese Subjects",infoboxKicker:"Protected interests, legal limits, and imperial obligation",
    lead:"The rights and duties of Japanese subjects are the constitutional and statutory claims attached to membership in Imperial Japan. They include legal process, property, petition, movement, religion, speech, publication, assembly, political participation, taxation, education, registration, and military obligation. Most constitutional rights operate within limits established by law, allowing genuine civic and parliamentary activity to coexist with managed opposition and unequal imperial citizenship.",
    canon:"Constitutional rights within law, managed elections, restricted anti-imperial politics, Diet influence, taxation and military obligation, Japanese nationality in the integral regions, metropolitan preference, coercive assimilation in Chōsen, and reform pressure against arbitrary administration are established. Exact franchise, rights statutes, emergency limitations, sex equality, regional representation, legal remedies, and 1985 civil-liberties doctrine remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"subjecthood",title:"Imperial subjecthood",html:"<p>Political membership does not rest on popular sovereignty. Subjects owe allegiance to the Emperor and receive legal status through the imperial constitution, statutes, nationality, household registration, and the institutions administering them.</p>"},
      {id:"rights",title:"Protected interests",html:"<p>Property, petition, lawful residence, religious profession, speech, publication, assembly, association, and due process possess constitutional recognition. Their practical strength depends on legislation, police practice, courts, parliamentary protection, and social position.</p>"},
      {id:"law",title:"The reservation to law",html:"<p>Rights are commonly protected within limits of law. Government can therefore regulate organizations, public meetings, publications, security information, residence, and emergency activity without formally denying that subjects possess rights.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Political participation",html:"<p>Elections, parties, factions, petitions, local assemblies, Diet investigations, newspapers, universities, and professional associations provide real political channels. Parties committed to overthrowing the imperial order or dissolving Japanese leadership of the Sphere face restriction or exclusion.</p>"},
      {id:"duties",title:"Taxation, education, and military service",html:"<p>Subjects contribute revenue, comply with registration and schooling, obey lawful administration, and meet military or alternative national obligations. These duties connect citizenship to the developmental and strategic capacity of the Empire.</p>"},
      {id:"inequality",title:"Unequal imperial citizenship",html:"<p>Common nationality does not create common power. Home-island Japanese possess advantages in language, schools, careers, officer promotion, courts, corporate leadership, and access to the center. Korean, Taiwanese, Ryukyuan, Karafuto, and Pacific subjects experience the same legal vocabulary through different institutions.</p>"},
      {id:"associates",title:"Associated-state citizens",html:"<p>Citizens of Thailand, the Philippines, Manchukuo, National China, and other associated states are foreign nationals, not Japanese subjects. Their rights in Japan arise from treaty residence, local law, contract, and reciprocity rather than imperial subjecthood.</p>"},
      {id:"reform",title:"Rights politics by 1985",html:"<p>Reformers seek cleaner elections, wider oversight, bounded police discretion, fair administration, regional representation, women's legal capacity, and credible remedies. Conservatives argue that rights survive because imperial order, social duty, and strategic guardianship prevent factional dissolution.</p>"},
      {id:"post-preservationist-rights",title:"Post-preservationist rights settlement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-r6m2v writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T11:44:47-06:00 --><p>By the mature postwar order, residents of Chōsen, Taiwan, Karafuto, the older Micronesian possessions, and later incorporated Pacific territories held Japanese nationality rather than parallel regional citizenships.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Their right to reside and work across integral Japan distinguished them from treaty residents and citizens of associated states.</p><p>Post-preservationist policy treated cultural protection as compatible with that common citizenship. Schools, archives, religious institutions, protected ceremonies, broadcasting, and voluntary heritage associations could receive legal support; their recognition did not automatically establish a right to political separation, an independent nationality, or exemption from common taxation and service.</p><p>Common citizenship was nevertheless formed through coercive assimilation, land seizure, language hierarchy, unequal schooling, police restriction, and metropolitan preference in corporate and administrative careers. Its capacity to support movement, employment, relief, and legal claims explains its durability. Administrative function alone does not establish that the settlement was equal or legitimate.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"constitution-empire-japan-after-1947.html",kicker:"Constitutional source",label:"Constitution after 1947"},{href:"administrative-law-imperial-japan.html",kicker:"State limitations",label:"Administrative Law"},{href:"civil-code-family-registration-imperial-japan.html",kicker:"Status and identity",label:"Civil Code and Family Registration"},{href:"political-opposition-postwar-japan.html",kicker:"Political boundary",label:"Political Opposition"},{href:"integral-territories-japan.html",kicker:"Unequal citizenship",label:"Integral Territories of Japan"},{href:"treaty-residence-geacps.html",kicker:"Foreign residents",label:"Treaty Residence in the Sphere"}],
    facts:[["Constitutional status","Rights of imperial subjects"],["General qualification","Within limits of law"],["Political channels","Elections, parties, Diet, assemblies, petition"],["Core duties","Taxation, registration, education, service"],["Integral-region status","Japanese nationality with unequal access"],["Associated-state citizens","Foreign nationals"],["Popular sovereignty","Not the constitutional basis"],["Complete 1985 rights doctrine","Open"]]
  })
