const addJapanLawSection=(slug,beforeId,section)=>{const article=window.deepArticles[slug];if(!article||article.sections.some(existing=>existing.id===section.id))return;const index=article.sections.findIndex(existing=>existing.id===beforeId);if(index<0)article.sections.push(section);else article.sections.splice(index,0,section);};
addJapanLawSection("constitutional-legal-order-imperial-japan","reform",{id:"authorization-chain",title:"Authorization and implementation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-k9qs3 writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:59:42-06:00 --><p>The mature constitutional order separated the authority to approve a course from the legal and material acts that made it operative. Imperial sanction supplied legitimacy at the constitutional apex. Cabinet coordination joined ministries and secured an executable policy. Diet legislation and supply authorized statutes, taxes, and expenditure. Ministries, courts, prefectures, municipalities, services, and regulated bodies produced the orders, judgments, records, contracts, and public works through which a subject encountered the decision.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Institutional level</th><th>Characteristic instrument</th><th>Operating limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Emperor, court, and exceptional conference</td><td>Appointment, sanction, rescript, treaty form, or reserved strategic settlement</td><td>Did not prepare every account, permit, route, appointment list, or enforcement file</td></tr><tr><td>Prime minister and Cabinet</td><td>Coordinated policy, ministerial instruction, legislative program, budget proposal, and crisis direction</td><td>Required money, law, service cooperation, and administrative execution</td></tr><tr><td>Imperial Diet</td><td>Statute, appropriation, tax, petition, investigation, and public ministerial accountability</td><td>Could not treat the throne, supreme command, or imperial settlement as ordinary partisan property</td></tr><tr><td>Ministry, service department, or public board</td><td>Ordinance, regulation, license, allocation, inspection, procurement, appointment, and benefit decision</td><td>Acted within constitutional, statutory, fiscal, and jurisdictional authority</td></tr><tr><td>Prefecture, territory, and municipality</td><td>Register, local budget, school, relief, police cooperation, land decision, road, water, sanitation, and emergency response</td><td>Worked beneath national supervision and unequal regional arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>Associated-state government</td><td>Treaty implementation, national statute, permit, concession, tax, judgment, and negotiated administrative act</td><td>Remained outside the Japanese constitution while bargaining under Japanese strategic and economic predominance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The postwar bargain preserved sacred imperial arbitration, Army and Navy roles, bureaucratic planning, zaibatsu property, Diet budgets, local offices, and associated-state ministries while limiting each institution's claim to govern the whole settlement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A constitutional account therefore has to identify the instrument as well as the prestige behind it. A court audience could influence a Cabinet; only the competent body could enter the appropriation, issue the permit, register the household, command the unit, or enforce the judgment.</p><p>Post-preservationist jurists favored this allocation because archives, schools, ceremonies, local grants, and administrative accommodations could be maintained without reopening sovereignty whenever a cultural claim arose. They judged a proposed reform by its ability to keep law, relief, transport, taxation, employment, and peace working through named institutions. That approach protected continuity while favoring offices already strengthened by Japanese-language records, metropolitan credentials, corporate capacity, police power, and earlier land acquisition. A functioning authorization chain could carry inherited coercion with great efficiency.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
addJapanLawSection("constitutional-legal-order-imperial-japan","reform",{id:"competence-consent",title:"Competence and consent",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-b-20260816-p7r2v writer=b kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T14:08:01-06:00 --><p>The authorization chain established which office could make an act legally effective. It did not by itself establish whose consent the act represented. Imperial sanction, a Diet appropriation, a prefectural order, a municipal register, and an associated-state permit answered different constitutional questions. Later implementation could confirm, resist, delay, or condition a policy within the implementing body's powers. It could not retrospectively supply consent to an incorporation or treaty settlement that the affected population had no equal power to accept or reject.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Evidence of formal competence</th><th>Evidence needed to assess effective consent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Integral territory</td><td>Japanese statute, budget, ministry order, territorial or prefectural act, municipal decision, and access to Japanese courts</td><td>Franchise, Diet and local representation, language access, police restriction, ability to organize, control of land and records, and whether opposition could contest status or only administration</td></tr><tr><td>Associated state</td><td>Treaty, national statute, ruler or Cabinet approval, permit, concession, tax decision, judgment, and appropriation under the state's own law</td><td>Accountability of the approving institution, public and legislative access to the bargain, lawful power to refuse or revise it, independent evidence, and capacity to preserve finance, security, employment, and essential services after refusal</td></tr><tr><td>Japanese implementing body</td><td>Appropriation, regulation, command, contract, corporate authority, or administrative delegation</td><td>Whether the body could alter terms, record objections, suspend performance, or report that political promises exceeded the law, money, or capacity supplied</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In an integral territory, voting for a municipality or petitioning a ministry could authorize a local representative and produce a real remedy. It did not amount to a continuing plebiscite upon Japanese sovereignty. Common nationality widened movement and access to services while the protected constitutional settlement limited the political consequences that regional opposition could seek.</p><p>In an associated state, a valid national permit or appropriation proved that the competent host institution had acted. The quality of the bargain depended upon whether ministers could obtain independent advice, disclose alternatives, enforce host law against Japanese firms and residents, and survive the withdrawal of Japanese credit, transport, expertise, or protection. Dependence did not make every act fictitious. It did require the record to distinguish negotiated assent, acceptance under constrained alternatives, administrative compliance, and popular approval.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
addJapanLawSection("administrative-law-imperial-japan","reform",{id:"decision-record",title:"Administrative decision record",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-fncw0 writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:59:42-06:00 --><p>Administrative legality depended upon the record connecting a general power to a particular result. The competent office had to identify the applicant or affected person, the land or activity, the legal authority, the relevant inspection or evidence, the fiscal or security consequence, the decision, and the official responsible for carrying it out. The archive leaves standing rules and review statutes open, but the diversity of permits, benefits, requisitions, police restrictions, and development decisions makes these distinctions necessary for any usable remedy.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Administrative act</th><th>Material required for review</th><th>Possible institutional response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>License, quota, or strategic allocation</td><td>Application, technical standard, available capacity, priority schedule, reasons, and comparable decisions</td><td>Agency reconsideration, ministerial supervision, Diet inquiry, statutory appeal, or judicial challenge where authorized</td></tr><tr><td>Land acquisition or public works</td><td>Survey, title, public purpose, route alternatives, valuation, occupants, compensation, and construction authority</td><td>Local hearing, revised design, compensation award, administrative appeal, political petition, or court action</td></tr><tr><td>Police or censorship restriction</td><td>Order, legal basis, identified risk, evidence, scope, duration, service record, and notice</td><td>Superior review, petition, legislative scrutiny, prosecution challenge, or other lawful remedy</td></tr><tr><td>School, welfare, pension, or registration decision</td><td>Household and residence records, eligibility rule, supporting certificates, calculation, and stated deficiency</td><td>Correction, benefit appeal, municipal or ministry review, and adjudication of status where available</td></tr><tr><td>Emergency requisition or movement control</td><td>Declared necessity, territorial scope, property or persons affected, command responsibility, duration, inventory, and compensation record</td><td>Renewal or termination, audit, compensation, investigation, and later legal or political review</td></tr><tr><td>Associated-state project</td><td>Treaty or concession, host permit, corporate contract, national law, tax position, labor terms, and strategic reservation</td><td>Host administrative process, bilateral negotiation, national court, company remedy, or interstate settlement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Miki-era reform joined public trust to review of major expenditure, clearer responsibility, local representation, compensation, and limits on concealed corporate influence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> It did not establish a single universal tribunal in the surviving record. Ministries, the Diet, courts, local bodies, prosecutors, auditors, professional associations, and the political press could examine different parts of an administrative failure. Naming the venue prevented a general complaint about arbitrary government from being mistaken for an already available legal remedy.</p><p>Post-preservationist administration preferred correction of the file, office, route, contract, or service over reconstruction of a former political jurisdiction. A cultural institution could seek custody, access, teaching funds, a protected ceremony, or a corrected record while nationality and strategic command remained intact. The requirement for legible evidence could exclude oral tenure, customary authority, minority-language testimony, displaced households, and communities whose documents had been created by the office they challenged. Procedure constrained power only to the extent that claimants could enter it and that reviewing institutions could enforce the result.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
